# Atomic Cohesive Types

When a type accumulates multiple responsibilities, modes, policies, algorithms, or state transitions, extract those concerns into small, named collaborators.

## Rules

- Prefer atomic types with one clear responsibility and one reason to change.
- Extract distinct behavior modes into cohesive collaborators when branching by state or mode becomes central to the type.
- Treat State, Strategy, Policy, Command, value objects, and small domain services as valid decomposition tools.
- Extract only when there is a concrete threshold: recurring branching on mode/state, or a responsibility that is independently testable. Do not force patterns onto trivial code.
- Keep extracted types cohesive, not just collections of thin helper methods. For wiring, see [composition-over-inheritance.mdc](.cursor/rules/composition-over-inheritance.mdc).

## Example

State Pattern is one valid decomposition when modes have distinct rules—not mandatory for every simulator-like type.

```swift
// ❌ BAD — one type owns every behavior mode
final class Simulator {
    func tick() {
        switch mode {
        case .pedaling: ...
        case .coasting: ...
        case .random: ...
        }
    }
}

// ✅ GOOD — explicit behavior objects when modes have distinct rules
final class Simulator {
    private var state: SimulatorState

    func tick() {
        state = state.tick()
    }
}

protocol SimulatorState {
    func tick() -> SimulatorState
}

extension Simulator {
    struct PedalingState: SimulatorState { ... }
    struct CoastingState: SimulatorState { ... }
    struct RandomState: SimulatorState { ... }
}
```

Use the smallest decomposition that makes the domain clearer.
